  /* The noise is the surface, so it reaches exactly as far as the surface
     does. Carried on into the margins it would leave them a shade off the page
     black they are meant to be indistinguishable from — `overlay` on a near
     black modulates it barely, but barely is not the same as not at all.

     After the rule above rather than beside the gradient it belongs with: both
     are `.footer::before` at the same specificity, so this only wins by coming
     later. */
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .footer::before {
      inset: 0 var(--grid-margin);
    }
  }

  /* Sized against the viewport rather than the column grid: it is a mark
     closing the page, not a piece of content in a column. Large enough to be
     the page's last statement — roughly half the width, which the four lines
     of copy beside it need nowhere near the rest of. The ceiling is what stops
     it growing without end on a very wide display.

     Drawn in the hairline ink rather than the accent, and from the outline cut
     of the mark rather than the solid one: at this size a filled acid-green
     monogram is the loudest thing on the page, and the footer is the quietest
     part of it. As an outline in the same grey as the rules it becomes another
     line on the grid — the largest one — rather than a logo sitting on top of
     it. Laid over the footer's own coat of that ink it reads one step brighter
     than the background, the same way a crossing pair of hairlines does. */
  .footer__logo {
    display: block;
    /* Width is what the mark is sized by, but height is what has to fit: it is
       all but square, so on a short window a width of 46vw would be taller than
       the screenful and push the rule above it out of view. The second term is
       the ceiling that keeps the whole footer inside one screen. */
    width: min(clamp(300px, 46vw, 700px), 70lvh);
    /* RiyoOutline's own 457 × 452, and it has to be the asset's ratio rather
       than near it: at any other, `contain` fits the mask to the tighter axis
       and centres it, and the letterboxing that leaves is a margin holding the
       mark off the rule below. The outline's ink runs to all four edges of its
       viewBox, so with the ratio matched its left stroke lands on the box's own
       left edge — which is the grid margin, which is where VerticalEdges draws
       its line. The mark meets the rule rather than nearly reaching it. */
    aspect-ratio: 457 / 452;
    background-color: var(--border-hairline);
    -webkit-mask: url("/RiyoOutline.svg") no-repeat center / contain;
    mask: url("/RiyoOutline.svg") no-repeat center / contain;
  }

  /* One block of copy, so the four lines are spaced by the body leading and
     nothing else — the copyright reads as the last line of the stack rather
     than as a separate footnote. */
  .footer__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-b2);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--ink-primary);
  }

  /* White, like the copy around it. The underline is the whole hover state:
     a colour change would make these the only links on the page that are not
     the ink they sit in. */
  .footer__link {
    position: relative;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* The underline is drawn rather than declared, because `text-decoration`
     has no left edge to animate — it is there or it is not. This is a rule of
     the site's own hairline weight that wipes on from the left, in the
     direction the name above it is read.

     It sits below the descenders rather than through them. `text-decoration`
     was stepping around them itself (skip-ink), and a drawn rule has no such
     manners; 0.1em clears the 0.25em descent at every size the clamped body
     type takes, and a hairline sitting clear under the word is this site's
     vocabulary anyway.

     `scaleX` on a 1px box, so the whole thing is one composited transform.
     The obvious alternative — growing `background-size` from 0% — repaints
     the text's own box every frame for the same picture.

     One duration for all three links rather than a constant speed: the email
     is three times the width of `LinkedIn`, and matching their speeds would
     make it the slow one. Sharing a duration is what makes them read as one
     set of links instead of three separate effects. */
  .footer__link::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0.1em;
    height: var(--border-hairline-width);
    background-color: currentColor;

    /* Held in a variable so the two states below say only which way it goes,
       and the reduced-motion case can take the movement out in one place. */
    --underline: 0;
    /* Retracting, which is the shorter of the two: the reader has already
       moved on, and an exit that takes as long as the entrance reads as the
       page being slow to let go. */
    --underline-duration: 160ms;

    transform: scaleX(var(--underline));
    /* Left, both ways. Swapping the origin on exit would sweep the line off
       to the right and keep every movement travelling the same way, which is
       prettier — but a hover broken off mid-draw would then jump the drawn
       part bodily across to the other end before it retracted. These are
       three links stacked a line apart, so a pointer crosses them in and out
       constantly and that interruption is the common case, not the edge. */
    transform-origin: left center;
    transition: transform var(--underline-duration) var(--ease-out-strong);
  }

  /* Steep enough that most of the width is covered early: the line reads as
     arriving, then settling, rather than travelling at one speed. */
  .footer__link:focus-visible::after {
    --underline: 1;
    --underline-duration: 260ms;
  }

  /* Gated: on touch a tap fires hover too, and the line would be left drawn
     under whichever link was last pressed. */
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .footer__link:hover::after {
      --underline: 1;
      --underline-duration: 260ms;
    }
  }

  /* The underline is the affordance, so it stays — what goes is the travel.
     It fades in place instead, which says the same thing without a hairline
     sweeping across the screen. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .footer__link::after {
      transform: none;
      opacity: var(--underline);
      transition: opacity 120ms ease;
    }
  }

  .footer__copyright {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
  }

  /* Side by side stops fitting once the mark and four lines of 20px copy have
     to share a phone's width, so the mark takes the whole of the grid's width
     on its own line and the copy goes underneath it. It keeps the left edge;
     what it gives up is being the last thing on the page. */
  @media (max-width: 699px) {
    .footer {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      row-gap: clamp(24px, 6vw, 40px);
      padding-bottom: clamp(40px, 8vw, 56px);
      /* Half a screen rather than a whole one. Stacked, the footer is tall
         enough on its own that a full screenful was mostly the empty stretch
         above the mark, and the hairline is not subtracted here because the
         rule only has to land on the viewport's top edge at full height. */
      min-height: 50vh;
      min-height: 50lvh;
    }

    /* Margin to margin, not edge to edge: the mark lines up with the copy
       under it and with every other section's content above it. */
    .footer__logo {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
